> Back to Tutorial Hub -> return_hub

# ─── Quick Reference ─────────────────────────────────────────────

@label quick_reference
[show lavender center talking]
Lavender: Camera work should direct attention:
narrator: {color=#8888cc}[camera pan 0,0 100,0 easeInOut 1000]{/color}
narrator: {color=#8888cc}[camera zoom 1.0 1.12 easeIn 600]{/color}
narrator: {color=#8888cc}[show lavender left talking]{/color}
narrator: {color=#8888cc}[show alex right neutral]{/color}
narrator: {color=#8888cc}[move lavender center easeInOut 400]{/color}
[show lavender center happy]
Lavender: Checklist: establish the scene, keep speakers readable, and reset camera moves when the moment ends.
> Start the lesson sections -> section_pan
> Back to menu -> lesson_menu

# ─── Camera Pan ──────────────────────────────────────────────────

@label section_pan
[show lavender left talking]
Lavender: {b}[camera pan x1,y1 x2,y2 easing duration]{/b} — slides the viewport across the scene.
narrator: {color=#8888cc}[camera pan 0,0 100,0 easeInOut 1000]{/color}
narrator: Pans from left to right over 1 second. Characters stay put — the world moves behind them.
[show lavender center talking]
Lavender: When to pan:
narrator: {b}Establishing shots{/b} — slowly reveal a new location before characters appear.
narrator: {b}Following a character{/b} — pair with [move] for a tracking shot.
narrator: {b}Shifting attention{/b} — pan toward a new character entering from offscreen.
Lavender: When {b}not{/b} to pan:
narrator: During emotional dialogue. Movement competes with the words. Let the scene be still.
[show lavender center happy]
Lavender: Reset the camera after panning: {color=#8888cc}[camera pan 100,0 0,0 easeOut 600]{/color}
> Next: camera zoom -> section_zoom
> Back to menu -> lesson_menu

# ─── Camera Zoom ─────────────────────────────────────────────────

@label section_zoom
[show lavender right talking]
Lavender: {b}[camera zoom from to easing duration]{/b} — changes the camera's magnification.
narrator: {color=#8888cc}[camera zoom 1.0 1.2 easeIn 600]{/color}
narrator: Zooms in 20% over 600ms. Subtle, but the player feels the scene become more personal.
[show lavender center talking]
Lavender: Zoom levels:
narrator: {b}0.8–0.9{/b} — wide shot. Establishing, showing the full scene, isolation.
narrator: {b}1.0{/b} — neutral. Default. Most dialogue lives here.
narrator: {b}1.1–1.2{/b} — close-up. Emotional beats, confessions, tension.
narrator: {b}1.3+{/b} — extreme close-up. Revelations, breakdowns. Use very sparingly.
[show lavender left talking]
Lavender: Always reset when the moment passes:
narrator: {color=#8888cc}[camera zoom 1.2 1.0 easeOut 400]{/color}
narrator: A lingering zoom that the player didn't ask for feels like a bug, not a cinematic choice.
[show lavender center happy]
Lavender: Even a 5% zoom (1.0 → 1.05) during dialogue creates a subtle sense of focus.
> Next: staging two characters -> section_two_char
> Back to menu -> lesson_menu

# ─── Two-Character Staging ───────────────────────────────────────

@label section_two_char
[show lavender center talking]
Lavender: The classic two-person conversation: one character on left, one on right.
narrator: {color=#8888cc}[show lavender left talking]{/color}
narrator: {color=#8888cc}[show alex right happy]{/color}
Lavender: Staging rules for two characters:
narrator: {b}Speaker active, listener neutral.{/b} Change the speaker's expression. Keep the other in a resting state.
narrator: {b}Side = role.{/b} The questioner is often on the left, the responder on the right. Swap sides to signal a power shift.
narrator: {b}Don't stack same-side.{/b} Two characters on the left reads as confusion.
[show lavender left talking]
Lavender: Use [move] to shift balance mid-scene:
narrator: {color=#8888cc}[move lavender center easeInOut 400]{/color}
narrator: Lavender steps to center — she's taking control of the conversation.
[show lavender center happy]
Lavender: Position changes tell the player something shifted before the dialogue confirms it.
> Next: composition -> section_composition
> Back to menu -> lesson_menu

# ─── Composition ─────────────────────────────────────────────────

@label section_composition
[show lavender center talking]
Lavender: Four principles that work for any scene:
narrator: {b}1. Rule of thirds.{/b} Place characters at 1/3 or 2/3 of the screen width. Dead center is for authority or isolation.
narrator: {b}2. Empty space carries meaning.{/b} A character alone on a wide shot reads as lonely. Fill or empty the frame deliberately.
narrator: {b}3. Match framing to mood.{/b} Close for intimate, wide for epic, off-center for unease.
narrator: {b}4. Background supports tone.{/b} A cluttered background behind a serious conversation is distracting.
[show lavender left talking]
Lavender: The test: play your scene, notice where your eyes go. If they go to the wrong place, adjust the staging.
[show lavender center happy]
Lavender: Stillness is underrated. Not every scene needs camera movement. Sometimes the best shot is the steady one.
> Practice: staged scenes -> practice_scene
> Back to menu -> lesson_menu

# ─── Practice ────────────────────────────────────────────────────

@label practice_scene
[show lavender center talking]
Lavender: Three staged scenes. Each uses camera and positioning differently. I'll annotate the script after each.

@label practice_demos
Lavender: {b}Scene 1: Confrontation{/b} — closing distance.
[show lavender far_left talking]
Lavender: We need to talk.
[move lavender left easeInOut 500]
[wait 500]
[show lavender left emphasis]
Lavender: Now.
[wait 300]
[show lavender center talking]
narrator: {color=#8888cc}[show far_left] → [move left 500ms] → [show emphasis]{/color}. The shrinking gap is the escalation. No camera needed.
[wait 400]

Lavender: {b}Scene 2: Confession{/b} — zoom in, slow down.
[show lavender center talking]
[textspeed 38]
Lavender: I should have told you sooner.
[wait 400]
Lavender: I'm sorry.
[textspeed 28]
[show lavender center happy]
narrator: {color=#8888cc}[textspeed 38]{/color} slowed the pace. A {color=#8888cc}[camera zoom 1.0 1.15 easeIn 800]{/color} here would close the emotional distance.
[wait 400]

Lavender: {b}Scene 3: Reveal{/b} — enter from nothing.
[hide lavender]
[wait 600]
narrator: The room was empty. Dust hung in the air.
[wait 400]
[show lavender far_right talking]
[move lavender center easeOut 500]
[wait 500]
[show lavender center happy]
Lavender: Surprise.
narrator: {color=#8888cc}[hide] → [wait] → narrator → [show far_right] → [move center easeOut]{/color}. The emptiness set up the entrance.

[show lavender center talking]
Lavender: Three scenes, three staging strategies: movement as tension, zoom as intimacy, absence as setup.
> Replay demos -> practice_demos
> Back to menu -> lesson_menu

